AU  - Melo, W.
TI  - Enhancing the MBSE Process with Agentic AI
PY  - 2026
T2  - SysCon 2026 - The 20th Annual IEEE International Systems Conference, Conference Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/SysCon66367.2026.11503504
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040702025?origin=resultslist
AD  - Mitre Corporation, United States
AD  - Georgetown University, United States
AD  - George Washington University, United States
AB  - This paper presents and empirically evaluates an agentic, multimodal AI pipeline that transforms informal, ad hoc diagrams into formal Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) artifacts. Early systems engineering activities often rely on unstructured sketches and office tool diagrams (e.g., Visio, PowerPoint, whiteboards), which lack formal metamodels and cannot be directly ingested by UML/SysML tools. The proposed approach addresses this long-standing gap by integrating multimodal large language models (MLLMs), few-shot in-context learning, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and standardsbased tooling into a coordinated multi-agent workflow. The architecture comprises a Transformer Agent that converts images of UML class diagrams into structured models and XMI for ingestion into tools such as Cameo, Sparx EA, and Visual Paradigm; a RAG-based Researcher Agent that enriches models using curated domain corpora; a Documenter Agent that generates plain-language documentation via constrained web retrieval; a Reviewer Agent that checks conformance to INCOSE-informed and domain-specific modeling practices; a Coordinator Agent that orchestrates tasks and maintains provenance; and a human-in-the-loop for validation and targeted corrective feedback. Validation was conducted on a public corpus of 67 handdrawn UML class diagrams, using five images as few-shot exemplars and 62 for evaluation. Two Transformer configurations were compared: GPT-5 and Qwen3-VL-235B-Instruct, both under identical prompting conditions. Across 253 classes, 357 attributes, and 219 relationships, GPT-5 achieved Precision =97%, Recall =96%, and F 1=0.96, while Qwen3-VL achieved Precision =92%, Recall =97%, and F1=0.94. These results demonstrate that a substantial portion of the diagram-to-model reconstruction process can be automated while preserving design intent, with trade-offs between selectivity and coverage. The paper discusses threats to validity, underscores the continued importance of human oversight, and outlines a research agenda that extends the agentic pipeline toward simulation, analysis, change management, and model governance within responsible assurance and evaluation frameworks.  © 2026 IEEE.
