AB  - UML state diagrams are increasingly used in secondary education, such as the Japanese Informatics I course, yet students often struggle with dynamic behavior even after mastering surface notation. Existing execution environments assume carefully prepared digital models in dedicated tools, which can be demanding for novices. This work-in-progress proposes an LLM- based approach that generates executable code directly from images of hand-drawn UML state diagrams, allowing students to experiment with runtime behavior while keeping the familiar practice of sketching by hand. We implement prototypes for smartphone and web environments and evaluate conversion accuracy on diagrams of varying complexity. Preliminary experiments show useful accuracy on low-complexity diagrams and confirm that the system does not silently fix intentionally faulty diagrams, suggesting that this approach is promising for supporting novices in learning dynamic behavior. © 2026 IEEE.
