AB  - In modern avionics, fault diagnosis of electronic components relies heavily on expert interpretation of complex multimodal technical datasheets that integrate text, schematics, and timing diagrams. This knowledge-intensive manual process is not only expertise-driven but also struggles to capture latent semantic relationships spanning across different document sections. Existing retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) frameworks fall short in parsing structured data and performing effective cross-modal inference between textual descriptions and waveforms, ultimately leading to diagnostic hypotheses that are factually inconsistent with the source material. To address this issue, we introduce the avionics diagnostic RAG (ADRAG) framework. ADRAG employs a hierarchical semantic tree with recursive partitioning and quality-gated multimodal assembly to aggregate related content across chapters, guiding the construction of a knowledge graph. Leveraging this dual structure, a multiagent system combines tree traversal for contextual navigation with graph queries for precise factual inference. To maintain retrieval robustness under structural knowledge deficits, an adaptive fallback mechanism is integrated directly into the pipeline. This hybrid approach ultimately optimizes the context provided to the large language model (LLM) for generating diagnostic hypotheses. Experiments on a specialized avionics dataset demonstrate that ADRAG significantly outperforms RAG baselines, especially in complex cross-modal reasoning tasks. Our framework helps engineers reliably diagnose faults in safety-critical domains by providing fact-based answers, improving the accuracy and verifiability of LLM-generated outputs. © 2026 IEEE. All rights reserved.
