AD  - University of Bari Aldo Moro, Department of Informatics, Bari, Italy
AD  - University of Guelph, Guelph, ON, Canada
AB  - This paper introduces a middleware system designed to make AI-generated feedback more transparent and actionable in educational contexts where students iteratively build structured artifacts, such as UML diagrams. Unlike traditional approaches that assess only the final product, our system incorporates behavioral traces to generate feedback grounded in the learner's process. The middleware is structured around four core dimensions: Localized Evidence, Reasoning Cues, Actionable Alternatives, and Process-aware Trace; and integrates techniques from process mining, structured action parsing, and retrievalaugmented generation. To measure transparency, we define seven automatic metrics capturing aspects such as traceability, timing, and factual grounding. In controlled scenario-based evaluations, our system consistently outperforms an LLM-only baseline across all key metrics, particularly in process alignment and action relevance. Although evaluated in software modeling, the approach generalizes to other domains where structured learning processes and expert models are available.  © 2026 IEEE.
