AB  - The manual creation of Unified Modeling Language (UML) Activity Diagrams from natural language software requirements is a time-consuming and error-prone process, leading to the need for automated approaches. Recent large language models (LLMs) have the capability to create activity diagrams from natural language descriptions, but they lack the ability to maintain structural and semantic correctness. This work explores the application of open-source LLMs for the automatic generation of executable PlantUML activity diagrams from natural language requirements. Three instruction fine-tuned models, namely Qwen 2.5-Coder-7BInstruct, Llama 3.1-8B-Instruct, and DeepSeek-R1-8B, are tested under baseline conditions and optimized using Supervised FineTuning (SFT) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) techniques. A structure-aware semantic evaluation framework is used to compare the generated activity diagrams with ground-truth diagrams based on action coverage, control-flow similarity (CFSim), concurrency coverage, loop coverage, and an aggregate similarity score. The experimental outcome indicates that fine-tuning is an effective method to improve the quality of the diagrams for all models. Among the LLMs tested, DeepSeek-R1-8B performs better in terms of accuracy in representing control-flow, concurrency, and loop constructs. The results clearly show that fine-tuning the models for the specific task leads to a significant improvement in the structural and semantic correctness of the automatically generated UML activity diagrams. © 2026 IEEE.
