AD  - KU Leuven, Faculty of Economics and Business, Naamsestraat 69, Leuven, 3000, Belgium
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities in natural language processing, but their ability to generate formal behavioral models from textual specifications remains understudied. This research-in-progress paper presents an evaluation of LLM-generated finite state machines (FSMs) from natural-language business case descriptions. We compare ChatGPT 5.2 and Claude Sonnet 4.5 across twelve business cases of varying difficulty using three prompting approaches to test different alterations of the descriptions of the business scenarios influence the results: unconstrained natural language (W1), focused natural language (W2) and iterative refinement (W3). FSMs are generated as PlantUML code and evaluated based on state and transition accuracy, semantic errors, process confusion, and self-transition detection. Results show that unconstrained prompting yields incomplete and inconsistent FSMs, particularly for complex cases, while prompts using redacted case descriptions augmented with iterative refinement achieve near-perfect accuracy. Prompting technique has a stronger impact on FSM quality than LLM choice. Common failure modes include process confusion, limited unhappy path coverage, and lack of self-transition detection. Our findings provide evidence-based guidelines for practitioners using LLMs in behavioral modeling tasks as well as future experimentation. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
