C7  - 29
DO  - 10.1145/3811427.3811441
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043970909?origin=resultslist
AD  - MIT Lincoln Laboratory, Lexington, MA, United States
AD  - National Laboratory of the Rockies, Golden, CO, United States
AB  - Exploring Knowledge Graphs (KGs) remains an open challenge, as doing so requires expertise in unconventional querying languages and reasoning over complex, evolving schemas. We present LinkQ, an agentic KG-exploration system that helps users refine natural language questions into structured queries through guided large language model (LLM) collaboration. LinkQ's design was informed by a formative study with KG experts, resulting in a human-in-the-loop interface that prioritizes transparency through visual mechanisms for verifying the agent's behavior and generated outputs. LinkQ has five primary components: an agentic state diagram that contextualizes each stage of the agent-querying pipeline, a query editor that pairs generated queries with LLM explanations, an entity-relation ID table showing extracted KG data with semantic descriptions, a query structure graph that depicts the path traversed within the KG, and an interactive, multi-attribute graph visualization of query results. We conducted a qualitative study with 14 KG practitioners to better understand how LinkQ impacts workflows, exploration strategies, and user trust. Our findings show that the interface design enables intuitive, effective KG exploration - even for users without prior querying expertise. At the same time, we observe risks of miscalibrated trust in agentic exploration systems, emphasizing the need for future work for trust calibration in LLM-driven analytic interfaces. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
