AD  - College of Computer Science and Technology, Tongji University, Shanghai, China
AB  - Context: Requirement modeling is a critical step in software development, especially in industrial manufacturing, where system failures often result from ambiguous or incomplete requirements. Traditional approaches struggle to accurately capture essential information from natural language descriptions, particularly complex relationships in use case diagrams beyond actors and use cases. Objectives: This study aims to leverage Large Language Models (LLMs) to automatically extract complex use case diagram elements from natural language text, addressing the limitations of conventional requirement modeling and improving its accuracy and efficiency. Methods: UCD-LLM employs LLMs guided by carefully designed prompts to extract use case diagram elements. It incorporates a self-reflection mechanism to mitigate hallucinations and introduces a multi-agent debate mechanism to emulate requirement modeler discussions, thereby enhancing the recognition of complex relationships among functional entities. Results: UCD-LLM is evaluated on the extraction of actors, use cases, and various relationships. Compared with a traditional machine-learning method and three zero-shot LLM baselines, it achieves superior results on most metrics. In five classic modeling cases, UCD-LLM improves FEF by 148%–167% over the traditional method. On the ReqUCD60 dataset, (Formula presented) attains an RNF of 0.693, 16.2% higher than the basic model Qwen. Ablation studies confirm that self-reflection and debate mechanisms substantially enhance model accuracy and robustness. Conclusion: UCD-LLM effectively captures use case diagram elements and their relationships from natural language texts. Built on pre-trained LLMs, it requires no complex architectures or large-scale training. Its strong performance and ease of deployment demonstrate a practical and scalable approach to applying LLMs in requirement modeling. The code is available athttps://github.com/UCD-LLM/UCD-LLM. © 2025 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
