AD  - MCKV Institute of Engineering, Liluah, Howrah, India
AB  - Unified Modeling Language (UML) is an universally accepted standard for communication within the life cycle phases of software development. Informal requirements expressed in natural languages transform into a formal specification when represented in UML notation, artifacts, and diagrams. UML simplifies by reducing efforts in translating the evolution of textual requirements out of its abstractness and vagueness into concrete code and software during these phases. In this work, automation in a semiformal mapping of natural language texts to the UML artifacts is addressed. An approach has been presented for the construction of one of an important type of structural category UML diagram, named the Class Diagram, from natural language processing of requirements description. The current work is motivated by the wide usage and popularity of natural languages in prompting queries to Large Language Models (LLM) in recent times to generate a code precisely and automatically from a specified set of requirements. It is assumed that the code generated from a class diagram would be a more accurate and precise map of the requirements in natural language, and such an approach could also extend to any other intermediate representations by diagrams from the UML domain. Towards construction of a class diagram, the present work scans the sentential form of the requirements, and applies Parts-of-speech (POS) tagging from the NLP domain to determine the tokens for the words. Subsequently, tokens forming the natural language sentences are parsed by the NLTK parser using phrase structure grammar of the English language. Dependence analysis is performed on the parse trees in iterations to determine the names representing the classes, methods, attributes, and relationships, to finalize a class diagram. © 2026 IEEE.
