AB  - The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into software and information system development creates new opportunities for automating modeling tasks. While prior research has examined LLMs’ technical ability to generate conceptual models, little is known about how different development methodologies affect the effectiveness and user experience of human-LLM collaboration. To address this gap, we conducted an empirical experiment with 39 groups of undergraduate computer science students to investigate how plan-driven and iterative approaches influence human-LLM collaborative modeling outcomes and perceived usability. Participants collaborated with an LLM (GPT or Gemini) to elicit requirements and create UML use case, class, and sequence diagrams, using their knowledge and understanding to refine the models. After completing the task, participants completed a usability questionnaire assessing perceived usefulness, ease of use, and overall satisfaction. An independent evaluator assessed the semantic quality of the final models in terms of validity and completeness. Results indicate that the iterative approach produced, on average, higher-quality models than the plan-driven approach, although differences were not statistically significant. Qualitative observations show that plan-driven participants struggled with upfront setup and model hallucinations, whereas iterative users faced challenges with context drift and model regression across collaboration cycles. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
