TI  - Structure-guided function-level code generation with LLMs via UML activity diagrams
PY  - 2026
T2  - Neurocomputing
VL  - 669
C7  - 132502
DO  - 10.1016/j.neucom.2025.132502
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105025583194?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Big Data and Software Engineering, Chongqing University, Chongqing, 401331, China
AD  - School of Software Engineering, Chongqing University of Posts and Telecommunications, Chongqing, 400065, China
AB  - Despite substantial advancements in function-level code generation powered by Large Language Models (LLMs), a fundamental challenge remains: reducing the uncertainty in the code generation process from requirements to enable LLMs to produce code that more accurately meets the requirements. In this paper, we introduce a novel framework for function-level code generation, named StructGen, which employs Unified Modeling Language (UML) activity diagrams as structural guidance for code generation. StructGen comprises two LLM-based collaborative roles operating across two phases: a Designer that infers the requirements and models the design schemes using UML activity diagrams, and a Coder that generates code guided by the design schemes, while the Designer revises designs and the Coder repairs code through test feedback. StructGen introduces the sequential, selective, and iterative structures of UML activity diagrams to logically organize the implementation process, thereby deepening the LLM's understanding of requirements and increasing the determinism of code generation from requirements. Experimental results demonstrate that StructGen outperforms all competitive baselines with relative improvements of 9.4 %–37.3 % and also surpasses all comparative LLMs. Furthermore, the UML activity diagram-based design schemes are equally effective in enhancing the performance of both the LLMs and baselines, achieving relative improvements of 2.1 %–112.1 %. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
