AD  - Kutaisi International University, Kutaisi, Georgia
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in generating code, making them valuable assistants for software engineers during implementation. However, it remains an open question whether LLMs can provide meaningful support before coding begins, in particular during design, when a system is structured into components. In this case study, we investigate the ability of state-of-the-art LLMs: ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, and Microsoft Copilot, to generate designs from natural-language requirements. The designs are represented as UML class diagrams. We evaluate them in terms of correctness and completeness. Our results show that given well-specified, complete requirements, LLMs can produce correct and visually coherent UML diagrams. However, LLMs fall short when required functionality or data are not explicitly specified or novel solutions need to be generated. The results highlight the continued need for human creativity, expertise and judgment in software design. This study provides the first evaluation of LLMs for design tasks, offering insights into the opportunities and limitations of AI-assisted software engineering. © 2026 IEEE.
